artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider two-dimensional van der Waals magnetic heterostructures built from the itinerant ferromagnet Fe3GeTe2 (FGT) and antiferromagnetic transition-metal phosphorus trichalcogenides, specifically the bilayer FGT/FePS3, the trilayer FePS3/FGT/FePS3, and the bilayers FGT/MnPS3 and FGT/MnPSe3. These systems exhibit enhanced low-temperature magnetization of the FGT layer and an exchange-bias (EB) effect, including a double-shifted hysteresis loop in some FGT/FePS3 stacks and a two-step magnetization-versus-temperature behavior near 100 K for FGT/MnPSe3. The antiferromagnets differ in their magnetic ordering type (e.g., zigzag Ising-like in FePS3 vs. Néel order in MnPS3/MnPSe3) and in the strength and sign of the interlayer magnetic coupling within the AFM.

Determine, by means of a combined theoretical and experimental analysis (e.g., first-principles electronic-structure calculations together with thickness- and temperature-dependent magnetometry), how each of the following physical mechanisms quantitatively controls the enhanced magnetism of the FGT layer and the observed exchange-bias behavior (magnitude of the EB field, coercivity, occurrence of double-shifted loops, and any anomalous temperature dependences):
(i) oxidation of the FGT surface and the resulting oxidized FGT sublayer;
(ii) the type of antiferromagnetic ordering of the AFM partner;
(iii) the intralayer/interlayer exchange coupling inside the AFM as a function of AFM thickness;
(iv) reduced dimensionality (monolayer to few-layer limit) of FGT and of the AFM;
(v) interfacial charge transfer between FGT and the AFM;
(vi) possible interfacial spin reorientation at the FGT/AFM interface (in particular near 100 K in FGT/MnPSe3).

Provide a predictive model that disentangles the contribution of each mechanism to the EB field and to the temperature- and AFM-thickness dependence of the magnetization in these three heterostructure families.

